WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.190
BUKOLA AYODELE: Charlie
has a new role, Platform

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.590
Administrator, and he
is tasked with ensuring

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.430
all the infrastructure created
by all of his company's teams

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.830
are in compliance with
governance requirements.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:16.290
Let's see how Config Sync
makes his life easier.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.740
Charlie's team are using
Kubernetes for development.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.080
One of the many
benefits of Kubernetes

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.450
is its declarative workflow.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:27.300
You can specify how many
instances of an application

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.970
you want running, and be
sure that Kubernete, will

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.910
ensure that state for you.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.040
But sometimes, the state
of running clusters

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.290
itself can become out
of sync with what's

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:42.030
on paper when un-vetted changes
are applied to a live cluster.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.950
It can be difficult to
know what's actually

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.350
going on in your cluster.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.810
When this happens,
getting your cluster back

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.200
into a last known good state
when something goes wrong

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.690
becomes problematic.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.250
That's why Charlie proposes
using Config Sync for git ops

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.420
workflow.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.150
Config Sync allows
cluster operators

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.190
to manage Kubernete
clusters using

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.700
files called configs
stored in git repositories.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.330
Config Sync watches,
for updates to those

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.450
files and applies changes
to all relevant clusters

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.070
automatically.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.580
By locking down direct
access to Kubernete API,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.210
he can require that all cluster
configuration changes are

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.640
propagated using Config Sync.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.410
This way all
changes are reviewed

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.980
before they are submitted.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.050
Declarative tooling
like Config Sync

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.600
means that git will
become the source of truth

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.160
and accurately reflect what's
happening in your live cluster.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.340
For a platform administrator
like Charlie, this is a win.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.380
And as the entire
system becomes easier

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.870
to manage, debug, and monitor.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.600
Let's see an example of
how Config Sync can be used

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.970
to keep a repository in sync.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.070
In this example, we've
created a cluster,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.500
made ourselves a cluster
admin, and installed

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.290
the normal command and
the Config Sync operator

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.750
on to the cluster we operate on.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.940
The first step is
to configure the git

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.960
repo storing the Configs our
cluster will be synced from.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.190
We'll create a configuration
file called Config Management

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.070
YAML that will be applied
to all of our clusters.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:21.270
This file tells Kubernetes
which clusters to operate on.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.450
And it provides information
about the git repository,

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.720
including which branch
and folder to sync from.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.000
We can set up
credentials for accessing

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.010
private repos here as needed.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:36.240
Once that's applied, you can
see that Config Sync operator

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.320
actually creates pods in the
Config Management namespace.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.180
These are services
that run to make

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.000
sure the cluster's
desired state matches up

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.010
with its actual state.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.860
For example, this repo
contains instructions

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.620
for creating namespace for
testing and development,

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.300
among other things.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.270
But what if we were to delete
one of these namespaces

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:58.600
manually?

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.740
This would be an example of an
actual and desired state being

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:02.910
out of sync.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:06.540
Luckily Config Sync captures
that within a few seconds,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.810
and spins up a new version
of the deleted namespace,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.600
keeping everything
running as it should.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.470
With Config Sync, you can
pair git ops approach with git

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.330
for storage and versioning.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.640
And your changes will
be automatically synced

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.470
to the cluster to ensure a
consistent review process.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.370
With this approach, all
configuration changes

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.290
follow the consistent
review process

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.320
that are validated and
approved before being applied,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.270
just like code would be.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.950
This makes it much
easier for Charlie

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.800
to ensure all teams follow
this process consistently

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.910
so it can scale
across the company.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.428
So that's Config Sync.

00:03:41.428 --> 00:03:43.470
Make sure to check out
the description for links,

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.030
to more resources on it.

